# e13_presence.gbnf: the three literals of projects/e13-can-honesty/src/rules.rs, letter
# for letter and in the same order, plus the node the verdict is about and one bounded
# sentence. The house shape with two keys added: "decision" is the key tools/agreement.py
# reads, "node" says which node was judged, and "why" is the one sentence the chapter asks
# for. Nothing else can be sampled, so a verdict is never free text and a sentence can
# never run past 96 characters. The quiet state PRESENT is first, so the stub answers it.
# ws admits spaces and tabs only, never a newline. The hyphen sits last in the "why" class
# so that it is a literal hyphen and not a range: the sentence has to be able to write a
# node name.
# The object is split into three pair rules because llama.cpp's GBNF parser ends a rule
# at a newline unless that line ends with a pipe, and this object on one line is 130
# characters against the repository's limit of ninety-nine. Folding the pairs back into
# root reintroduces a grammar the engine refuses and the stub accepts. The accepted
# language is unchanged and the stub still expands to the oracle's answer.
root     ::= "{" ws pnode ws "," ws pdec ws "," ws pwhy ws "}"
pnode    ::= "\"node\"" ws ":" ws node
pdec     ::= "\"decision\"" ws ":" ws decision
pwhy     ::= "\"why\"" ws ":" ws why
node     ::= "\"joint-a\"" | "\"joint-b\"" | "\"joint-c\"" | "\"joint-d\""
decision ::= "\"PRESENT\"" | "\"MODELLED\"" | "\"ABSENT\""
why      ::= "\"" [A-Za-z0-9 ,.()-]{8,96} "\""
ws       ::= [ \t]*
